data.content:
**What happened:** In C# 14 / .NET 10, new span conversions cause `array.Contains(num)` to resolve to `MemoryExtensions.Contains(Span<T>, T)` instead of `Enumerable.Contains<T>(IEnumerable<T>, T)`. The `MemoryExtensions` overload **cannot be represented in an expression tree**, so it throws `NotSupportedException` at runtime (or at compile time in non-lambda contexts).

**Fix:** Cast the array to `IEnumerable<T>` or use `.AsEnumerable()` so the compiler picks `Enumerable.Contains`:

```csharp
// Option 1: .AsEnumerable()
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => array.AsEnumerable().Contains(num);

// Option 2: Explicit static call
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => Enumerable.Contains(array, num);
```

Both approaches force the compiler to bind to the LINQ `Enumerable.Contains` overload, which is representable in expression trees.
